Done. `/app/model.xml` is written; `/app/model_ref.xml` is untouched.

**What I found**

Profiling the reference (42 bodies, 41-capsule cable, one 3-dof `connect` equality, no contacts) showed ~263 µs/step, split as:

| stage | ref | tuned |
|---|---|---|
| position (inertia/BVH/constraints make) | 53 µs | 67 µs |
| velocity | 12 µs | 12 µs |
| **constraint solve** | **151 µs** | **4.5 µs** |
| advance (integration) | 42 µs | 42 µs |

The entire overhead was the Newton solver (`opt.solver=2`, the default). The constraint problem here is only 3 dofs, yet Newton's constraint stage dominates the step. I verified `iterations` has no effect (it exits early either way), and that the timestep could not be raised — `dt=0.004` is 0.51× fast but diverges to ~1e-3, well past `atol=1e-5`.

**The change** — one line added after `<compiler>`:

```xml
<option solver="PGS" tolerance="1e-10"/>
```

PGS on this tiny LCP converges to the same solution as Newton; the tight `tolerance` makes them agree to ~1e-10 per step. No mass, inertia, geometry, damping, joint, equality, contact or actuator values were modified — I diffed every physical field of the compiled models (`body_mass/inertia/ipos`, `dof_damping/armature`, `geom_*`, `eq_*`, `actuator_*`, `qpos0`, gravity/timestep/integrator/cone/solimp/solref) and they are bit-identical.

**Verification**

`python eval.py` passes repeatedly:
```
Final state difference: 0.0000
Speedup: 2.04x      Time pctg: 0.49
```
Beyond the eval's own seeds I swept 70 random seeds: worst element-wise state difference **4.7e-7** (20× inside `atol=1e-5`), all states finite, mean time ratio 0.488–0.495 (need ≤0.60). Since the margin is 18–20% on time and ~20× on accuracy, this should hold on a fresh install of the same MuJoCo release.
